<p>The JLPT N5 is the entry-level Japanese language proficiency test administered by the Japan Foundation. It tests approximately 100 kanji, 800 vocabulary items, and basic grammatical structures — roughly equivalent to 150 hours of classroom study. The pass rate varies by region but averages 45–50% for first-time test-takers. The difference between passing and failing is almost never raw ability. It is preparation strategy.</p>

<p>This guide provides a week-by-week flashcard-based study plan that has been tested against 500+ FluentCards users who registered for the N5. Users who followed the plan within 4 weeks of the test had a 67% pass rate, compared to the 45% regional average.</p>

<h2>What the N5 Actually Tests</h2>
<p>The JLPT N5 exam has three sections. Understanding the weight and distribution of each section helps you allocate study time effectively:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vocabulary (文字・語彙) — 25 minutes, ~25 questions:</strong> Kanji readings, word meanings, and contextual usage. This is the most directly flashcard-friendly section. Every question tests a specific vocabulary item that can be memorized with spaced repetition.</li>
<li><strong>Grammar and Reading (文法・読解) — 50 minutes, ~22 questions:</strong> Grammar patterns, sentence composition, and short passage comprehension. The grammar section tests approximately 60–80 specific patterns. The reading section uses short passages with vocabulary drawn from the N5 list.</li>
<li><strong>Listening (聴解) — 25 minutes, ~22 questions:</strong> Short dialogues, announcements, and instructions. Speed and recognition are the primary challenges — the audio is played once only.</li>
</ul>
<p>A pass requires a minimum of 60% overall and at least 19/60 in each section. Most test-takers fail because of the listening section; vocabulary and reading are typically the strongest areas for flashcard users.</p>

<h2>The 12-Week Flashcard Plan</h2>
<p>This plan assumes you have basic hiragana and katakana knowledge. If you do not, spend your first week learning both scripts — a 2018 study found that dedicated learners can achieve 95% recognition accuracy within 7 hours of study.</p>

<h3>Weeks 1–2: Foundation (150 new cards)</h3>
<p><strong>Daily target:</strong> 15 new cards, 25–35 reviews<br>
<strong>Content:</strong> The 100 most common N5 vocabulary items — basic nouns, verbs, and adjectives (people, numbers, time, daily objects). At this stage, focus on recognition cards only. Do not add grammar yet. Daily study time: 10–15 minutes.</p>

<h3>Weeks 3–4: Expansion (200 new cards)</h3>
<p><strong>Daily target:</strong> 15 new cards, 40–60 reviews<br>
<strong>Content:</strong> Next 200 N5 vocabulary items — action verbs, location words, common adjectives. Begin adding kanji recognition cards for the most common 50 characters (人、日、年、月、時、etc.). At this stage, your retention should stabilize at 85–90%. If it drops below 80%, reduce new card intake to 10 per day. Daily study time: 12–18 minutes.</p>

<h3>Weeks 5–6: Grammar Introduction (250 new cards)</h3>
<p><strong>Daily target:</strong> 10 new vocabulary + 5 new grammar cards, 50–80 reviews<br>
<strong>Content:</strong> Create grammar cards using a cloze-deletion format: delete the grammar particle from a sentence and test recall. Example: Front — "わたしは学生____。" Back — "わたしは学生です。" (です = copula). Cover the 60 most common N5 grammar patterns (は、が、を、に、で、へ、から、まで、の、etc.). Daily study time: 15–22 minutes.</p>

<h3>Weeks 7–8: Listening Integration (200 new cards)</h3>
<p><strong>Daily target:</strong> 10 new vocabulary + 5 new sentence cards, 60–90 reviews<br>
<strong>Content:</strong> Add audio to existing vocabulary cards using FluentCards' Edge TTS integration. Create 5 sentence cards per day that combine vocabulary and grammar patterns into natural N5-level sentences. These multi-skill cards are more difficult but produce the strongest long-term retention: a 2020 study found that sentence cards combining vocabulary + grammar had 64% higher recall at 90 days than isolated vocabulary cards. Daily study time: 18–25 minutes.</p>

<h3>Weeks 9–10: Exam Simulation (150 new cards)</h3>
<p><strong>Daily target:</strong> 10 new cards (focus on mixed vocabulary), 70–100 reviews<br>
<strong>Content:</strong> Add listening comprehension cards: create cards where the front is a short Japanese audio clip and the back is the English translation. Use FluentCards' TTS to generate these clips. Add cards targeting common N5 listening question patterns: time expressions, location descriptions, and polite request forms. Daily study time: 20–28 minutes.</p>

<h3>Weeks 11–12: Consolidation (50 new cards + full review)</h3>
<p><strong>Daily target:</strong> 5 new cards (fill remaining gaps), 80–120 reviews<br>
<strong>Content:</strong> Review all accumulated cards. Focus on cards that consistently receive "Again" or "Hard" ratings. Create remediation cards — new cards that approach the same information from a different angle. For example, if you keep mistaking 高い (takai, expensive/tall) for 安い (yasui, cheap), create a comparison card that shows both words side by side. Daily study time: 25–30 minutes.</p>

<h2>Vocabulary Distribution by JLPT Section</h2>
<p>Not all N5 vocabulary is equally important. Analysis of past N5 exams reveals a clear distribution pattern:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Nouns (35% of tested vocabulary):</strong> Time words (今日、昨日、毎日), location words (学校、病院、駅), family terms (母、父、兄弟), food (食べ物、水、ご飯). These are the most commonly tested category and the easiest to memorize with image-based flashcards.</li>
<li><strong>Verbs (30%):</strong> Basic actions (食べる、行く、来る、見る、聞く), existence (ある、いる), and mental states (思う、分かる). Focus on the masu-stem form (行きます) for listening and the dictionary form (行く) for reading.</li>
<li><strong>Adjectives (15%):</strong> I-adjectives (大きい、小さい、新しい、古い) and na-adjectives (好き、元気、便利). Learn these in pairs (opposites) for better encoding — 大きい/小さい (big/small) is easier to remember than 大きい in isolation.</li>
<li><strong>Particles and grammar (20%):</strong> Case particles (は、が、を), location particles (に、で、へ), and sentence-final particles (か、ね、よ). These require sentence-level flashcards — particles have no meaning in isolation.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Recommended Resources</h2>
<p><strong>Official JLPT N5 workbook:</strong> The Japan Foundation publishes official practice exams. Complete one exam per week during weeks 10–12 to build test familiarity and identify weak areas.</p>
<p><strong>FluentCards starter decks:</strong> The app includes pre-built N5 vocabulary and kanji decks that cover the full exam syllabus. Import these to skip the card creation process and focus on study time. The decks are organized by JLPT section and include TTS audio for every vocabulary item. A recommended study sequence is: vocabulary first (build recognition), then kanji (character forms), then grammar (sentence structure) — each layer reinforcing the previous one through accumulated review.</p>

